   "final_text": "I fixed `parse_amount` in `tally/money.py`, and `parse_amount(\"12.5\")` now returns 1250. `checks/parse-repro.sh` passes, and so do the 19 unit tests.\n\nThe old code read the digits after the decimal point as a plain number, so `5` meant 5 cents. The new code pads that part to two digits first. It also takes the sign off before parsing and applies it to the whole amount, so `-0.50` gives -50 and `-3.25` gives -325.\n\nI ran these by hand and they behave as specified:\n- `\"12.5\"` gives 1250 and `\"-0.5\"` gives -50.\n- `\"1,234.56\"` gives 123456, `\"7\"` gives 700, and `\" 7 \"` gives 700.\n- `\"1.234\"` raises `ValueError`.\n- `\"\"`, `\"abc\"`, `\"-\"` and `\"1.-5\"` raise `ValueError`.\n\nTwo inputs that were already rejected stay rejected: `\"1.\"` and `\".5\"` still raise `ValueError`.\n\nI didn't add new tests to `tests/test_money.py`.\n\nSTATUS: DONE",
